WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.413
Indigenous Procurement Fraud.

00:00:02.363 --> 00:00:09.090
In March 2026, Procurement Ombudsman Alexander Jeglic released what he called his most shocking report.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:17.159
Non-Indigenous businesses were using Indigenous-owned shell companies to access federal set-aside contracts meant exclusively for Indigenous businesses.

00:00:17.159 --> 00:00:22.272
Three of twenty-seven examined suppliers were not even in the Indigenous Business Directory.

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:27.113
Indigenous Services Canada says full reform will take until 2028.

00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:30.431
The Jeglic Report, March 2026.

00:00:30.431 --> 00:00:37.590
Procurement Ombudsman Alexander Jeglic examined a sample of twenty-seven suppliers who received Indigenous set-aside contracts.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.431
Three were not even listed in the Indigenous Business Directory.

00:00:41.431 --> 00:00:49.306
Non-Indigenous businesses were using Indigenous-owned shell companies as fronts to access contracts reserved for Indigenous economic development.

00:00:49.306 --> 00:00:53.465
The Ombudsman described it as his most disturbing finding in office.

00:00:53.465 --> 00:00:55.715
The procurement fraud pattern.

00:00:55.715 --> 00:00:58.329
This is not an isolated failure.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:02.193
ArriveCAN: ninety-three million to a two-person firm.

00:01:02.193 --> 00:01:06.137
Phoenix Pay: two point two billion in cost overruns.

00:01:06.137 --> 00:01:10.602
Indigenous set-aside exploitation: shell companies gaming the system.

00:01:10.602 --> 00:01:20.556
In every case, the Auditor General or Procurement Ombudsman documents the failure, the government promises reform, and the reform takes years to implement — if it's implemented at all.

00:01:20.556 --> 00:01:24.238
The pattern is: document, promise, delay, repeat.
